# Design system first

UI in this repo comes from `@elirobinson/react` (components, hooks), `@elirobinson/tokens` (color, type, space, radius, shadow, motion), and `@elirobinson/ai-patterns` (UI contracts and working patterns).

Upstream: https://github.com/EliRobinson/design-system

## Discover before you build

A remembered or documented component list goes stale on every design system release, so ask the installed packages:

```bash
pnpm ds                  # components (+ exports & variants), hooks, typography classes, token groups
pnpm ds props <Name>     # props and variant unions; accepts `Card` or `molecules/Card`
pnpm ds tokens [filter]  # tokens and values
pnpm ds classes [filter] # CSS classes the design system ships
pnpm ds contracts        # machine-checkable UI rules to satisfy
pnpm ds patterns         # working principles, including the definition of done
pnpm ds prompts [name]   # prompt templates
pnpm ds check            # known traps that fail with no error; run after setup and every upgrade
```

`pnpm exec elirobinson-ds` is the same command if the `ds` script is not wired up. Deeper detail: `node_modules/@elirobinson/react/src/components/`, `node_modules/@elirobinson/tokens/src/tokens.css`, `node_modules/@elirobinson/react/dist/manifest.json`.

## Rules

- Import each component by its full subpath; `pnpm ds props <Name>` prints the exact line. There is no barrel export and a bare package import does not resolve.
- Satisfy `pnpm ds contracts`. Each constraint carries its own `check` and a `verifiedBy` naming what enforces it.
- Set appearance with a component's own `variant` / `size` props. Use utility classes for layout (grid, flex, spacing) only, because the design system owns the look.
- Colors, radii, shadows, durations, font sizes come from tokens: mapped utilities, `.t-*` typography classes, or `var(--token)` in arbitrary values. Do not use literal values.
- On Tailwind v4, order the CSS entry as `@import 'tailwindcss'`, then the design system stylesheet (`@elirobinson/react/styles.css`, or `@elirobinson/tokens/tokens.css` for an app that does not import `styles.css`), then `@import '@elirobinson/tokens/tailwind.css'`. That last import is what makes `bg-background`, `text-muted-foreground`, `border-border` and friends resolve to tokens.
- Dark mode is `[data-theme="dark"]` (`.dark` also works). With `next-themes`, set `attribute="data-theme"`.
- Token overrides go in an unlayered `:root` block. `tokens.css` is unlayered, so an override inside `@layer base` silently loses to it. With `next/font`, re-point the families through `--ds-font-sans-override` / `--ds-font-mono-override` instead, with the font class on `<html>`.
- Import `@elirobinson/react/styles.css` once, in the app shell, not per component. It already imports `@elirobinson/tokens/tokens.css`; importing `tokens.css` as well bundles it twice. An app that does not import `styles.css` (no React, or per-component sheets only) imports `@elirobinson/tokens/tokens.css` itself.
- Heroes, page headers, empty states, sidebars are compositions of primitives, not missing components.

## UI copy

Functional copy (errors, empty states, helper and hint text, toasts, labels, button text, tooltips, confirmations, validation) is chrome. State the fact, then the consequence, then the action, and stop.

- No unverifiable frequency claims ("almost always", "this rarely happens").
- No blame attribution ("on their side", "check your connection").
- No filler pacing ("in a moment", "hang tight").
- No unprompted reassurance or apology ("don't worry", "we'll sort it out"). Reassurance is fine when it answers a question the reader is actually asking, and then it is a fact: "You have not been charged."
- No escalation paths nobody asked for, and no enthusiasm or exclamation marks.
- Past two short sentences, it is explaining, reassuring, or selling, so cut it back.

This rule covers chrome only, not this product's editorial voice. Marketing prose, conversational surfaces, and written deliverables are content, and their voice is deliberate. Where a surface mixes the two, the chrome follows this rule and the content is left alone.

`@elirobinson/eslint-config` reports the literal phrases, scoped to copy props and chrome components; it does not read ordinary prose. It ships at `warn` so an upgrade cannot fail a build. Raise it to `error` once your copy is clean: `designSystem({ copy: { severity: 'error' } })`.

## Missing something?

1. Compose from existing primitives.
2. Pull in the repo's sanctioned gap-filler and restyle it with tokens.
3. Hand-roll from tokens as a last resort, and flag it as a design system gap worth upstreaming.

MUI, Chakra, Ant Design, Mantine, HeroUI, Headless UI, DaisyUI, direct Radix imports, and bare `@elirobinson/*` imports are blocked by `@elirobinson/eslint-config`. Pick a rung above instead of working around it.

## Done

Run `pnpm ds patterns` and work through the Definition of Done for UI work checklist. It lives in the package so it can't go stale here.
